186045417] [17592186197976 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186197976 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186197976 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "401f21c4-4ada-5f5e-b68f-b4edd51e55ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186197977 #edb/ref 1080 "18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186197977 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186197977 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4d850692-8440-57f8-a931-14cb51d6f903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186198003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186198004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186198005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186198006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198002 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186198007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198002 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198002 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198002 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186198006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198002 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dd5a22f3-e62c-5edf-b899-6619cb4a4071" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198003 #edb/ref 1080 "-40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198003 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198003 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "940363d2-e432-556a-b0c0-2c42895c1117" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198004 #edb/ref 1080 "-24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198004 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198004 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "20e54280-ff88-5af2-8610-6ef05e375392" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198005 #edb/ref 1080 "-32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198005 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198005 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2740310c-d926-5b3d-916c-6755f45ae1b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198006 #edb/ref 1080 "-48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198006 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198006 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5bdb73f9-d522-5716-a40d-01eb373017ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198007 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198007 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198007 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "70c9a7e7-b1ad-5149-9703-aa84c63a4445" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198047 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198047 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + y$ given that $2x^2 + 3y^2 \\leq 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198047 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb947756-3471-59c8-b3c4-9b21a1a91557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198047 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,1⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= 2x^2 + 3y^2  = 1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,6y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x &=4\\lambda x \\\\ 1 &=6\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ \\lambda y &=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  = 1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+3{y}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda = \\dfrac12$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}y = \\dfrac{1}{6} \\qquad \\Rightarrow \\qquad y = \\dfrac1{3}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+3{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left(0,\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3} \\right), \\qquad  \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left(0, \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(0, -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt3}\\right)$ | $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $-\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3}$ | $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}, \\dfrac13 \\right),$ and its maximum value is $\\dfrac23.$" #edb/ref 17592186576411] [17592186198240 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8690" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198240 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y) = 3x^2 - y^2$ given that $3x^2 + 2y^2\\leq 16.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198240 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d0da02a-d686-5bb1-ab44-00286ab01783" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198240 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨6x,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=\\left(0,  0 \\right).$ This point belongs to our region.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= 3x^2 + 2y^2  = 16.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y), \\qquad g(x,y)=16\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨6x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨6x,4y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6x &=6\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ y(2\\lambda +1) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)  =16$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & =16\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0 \\right).$\n\n- Note that assuming $\\lambda = 1$ from the first equation leads to $y=0,$ which we already have a solution for, and $\\lambda = -\\dfrac12$ from the second equation leads to $x=0,$ which we also have a solution for. So, we have found all the candidates on the boundary.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n\\left( 0,0 \\right), \\qquad  \\left( 0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right), \\qquad \\left(\\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0\\right).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $\\left( 0,0 \\right)$ | $\\left( 0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2 \\right)$ | $\\left( \\pm \\dfrac{4\\sqrt 3}{3}, 0 \\right)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $-8$ | $16$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $\\left(0, \\pm 2\\sqrt 2  \\right),$ and its minimum value is $-8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186198241 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198241 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, find the maximum value of the sum of cosines of the angle measures of a triangle, given that all three angles are acute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198241 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9235c493-f092-5f14-b989-be3b4548a90d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198241 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the measures of the angles. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = \\cos{x}+\\cos{y}+\\cos{z},\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint\n\n$$\nx+y+z=\\pi.\n$$\n\nSince the angles are acute, we also have the constraints $0 \\lt x,y,z \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2.$ \n\nLet $g(x,y,z) = x+y+z.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\lambda \\nabla g(x,y,z), \\qquad g(x,y,z) = \\pi\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x &=\\lambda \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡y &=\\lambda \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡z &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sine function is one-to-one on the interval $\\left( 0, \\dfrac \\pi 2\\right),$ we can conclude that $x = y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $z=y =x.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+z & =\\pi  \\\\ x+x+x & =\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy to show that this point is indeed the maximum and not the minimum. To do that, we can compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right) \\\\  & ≈1.47 \\\\  & <f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186576411] [17592186198366 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198366 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186199017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198366 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186199022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186