ems in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198367 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198367 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "849449fb-bb08-54a3-b8ea-9f33ddd7a3e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186199016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186363472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Extreme Values: Cases With No Inner Critical Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198405 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9bbc1e6a-c728-5c5a-84f8-06a92c30a023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198494 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198494 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198494 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198494 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198494 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198494 #edb/ref 1045 "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198494 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186199326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198494 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198494 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198494 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e65e5013-8e69-5be7-a53f-8247c1f26c90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186197546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198946 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198946 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198946 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-2y$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad6a612f-7b80-5294-8d9b-5ae88e52a2b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198946 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨x,-2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=9\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨x,-2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2 &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1-2\\lambda ) &=0 \\\\ \\lambda y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=9$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 3).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}y=-1\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=9$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(-2)^{2}=9\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{5},-2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 3),\\,(\\pm \\sqrt{5},-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(0,-3)$ | $(\\pm \\sqrt{5},-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-6$ | $6$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,3),$ and its minimum value is $-6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186199263 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199263 #edb/ref 1061 32292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199263 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c833d29b-43fd-5a3a-83ae-7c9928c184c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199263 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199264 #edb/ref 1061 32293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199264 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b82b3836-2ed6-522e-8a6b-e9301766e38d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199264 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199326 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199326 #edb/ref 1061 44831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199326 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7bbfb776-26d8-5be7-abb8-cae6bab3294c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199326 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199327 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199327 #edb/ref 1061 44833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199327 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0e5ab67d-1254-5853-868a-8e8a117b0032" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199327 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199484 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find the minimum and maximum values of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to constraint $g(x,y) = x^2+y^2 \\leq 1.$ In other words, we wish to find the global extrema of $f(x,y)$ defined on the disc of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5ac34145b3911b19eb3775a915e8f0462c78502be6d6292e7682265c86eafce7.png)\n\n  \n\nTo find the extrema, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the critical points of $f$ inside the domain $x^2+y^2 < 1.$\n\n- Then, we find the extreme values of $f$ subject to the constraint $x^2+y^2=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers.\n\n- Finally, we determine the maximum and minimum values by evaluating $f$ at the candidate points.\n\nRecall that the critical points of a function $f(x,y)$ are the solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ Now, since the derivative of $f$ is the transpose of its gradient $\\nabla f,$ the critical points are also given by the solutions to $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0.$\n\nSo, we can find the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ Note that this point belongs to our domain.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + y^2  = 1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1), \\qquad (0,0).\n$$\n\nNote that we add the critical point $(0,0)$ to the list of the candidates above.\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(\\pm 1,0)$ | $(0, \\pm 1)$ | $(0, 0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $0$ at the point $(0, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at the points $(0, \\pm 1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199484 #edb/ref 1065 7743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199484 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7cc22f9f-d1be-570d-acd7-d3f2e0af7a51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199484 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228953 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6617" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228953 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+y=\\tan{x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan x \\sin x\\,\\textrm{d}x\n= -\\sin x + \\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228953 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "533cdb5e-1d90-5aae-961b-4fe7daa30b0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228953 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x)=-\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x)=\\displaystyle\\int\\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=\\tan x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x)=A\\cos x + B\\sin x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x)=\\cos x,\n\\qquad\ny_2(x)=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x| \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-ln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|)+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\,\\underbrace{-\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡xln\\,|\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x|}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-\\cos x\\ln\\!\\left|\\tan x+\\sec x\\right|.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186576411] [17592186228954 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6618" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228954 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny'' - 2y' + y = 18x e^{x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x} + g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. What could be the function $g(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228954 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7e995ec-7c1a-510d-be52-5ef3ce5f77aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186228954 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$. Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution $y_p$ may be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $u_1$ and $u_2$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\nu_1(x) = -\\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_2(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n\\qquad\nu_2(x) = \\int \\dfrac{f(x)y_1(x)}{W(y_1,y_2)}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c(x) = A e^{x} + B x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = e^{x}, \\qquad y_2(x) = x e^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{x}+x{e}^{x})-({e}^{x})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}+x{e}^{2x}-x{e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are independent.\n\nNow compute $u_1(x)$ and $u_2(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot x{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-18\\int {x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{18x{e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =18\\int x\\,dx \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-6{x}^{3}){e}^{x}+(9{x}^{2})(x{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-6{x}^{3}{e}^{x}+9{x}^{3}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+Bx{e}^{x}+\\underbrace{3{x}^{3}{e}^{x}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x) = 3x^3 e^{x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186576411] [17592186229140 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229140 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229140 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Real Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229140 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186228954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229140 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b165305b-42f4-584a-9f8f-9455e5a05a9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229141 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186229711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229141 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229141 #edb/ref 1039 "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229141 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186228953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229141 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229263 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229263 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186230000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229263 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186230001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229263 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229263 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Second-Order ODEs Using Variation of Parameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229263 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186229999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229263 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229263 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "452d066f-9aab-563c-a587-2ed3d81298e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229999 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229999 #edb/ref 1061 591922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229999 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cbfa3100-96a1-5e81-8141-ae263cd302ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229999 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230000 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230000 #edb/ref 1061 591923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230000 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8f214df2-6ee3-5385-9094-45bf89f34516" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230000 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230001 #edb/ref 1061 591926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230001 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "15420a9f-3be5-50bf-a481-21a048c336e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230001 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230244 #edb/ref 1064 "In this topic, we'll derive a formula for variation of parameters for second-order linear ODEs.\n\nSuppose we're given the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + p(x)y' + q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nand let $y_1$ and $y_2$ be fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation.\n\nRecall that the method of variation of parameters begins by assuming that a particular solution satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} &={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{p}^{'} &={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{'}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUsing these constraints, we will set up a system of two equations that we can solve for $u_1$ and $u_2.$\n\n- To obtain the first equation, we differentiate the constraint for $y_p$ given above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{'} & =({u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2})^{'} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{'}{y}_{1}+{u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}^{'}{y}_{2}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{'},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand since we assumed that $y_p' = u_1 y_1' + u_2 y_2',$ we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{'} & ={u}_{1}^{'}{y}_{1}+{u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}^{'}{y}_{2}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{'} \\\\ 0 & ={u}_{1}^{'}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{'}{y}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To obtain another equation, we substitute the constraints into the differential equation and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{''}+p(x){y}^{'}+q(x)y & =f(x) \\\\ ({u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{'})^{'}+p(x)({u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{'})+q(x)({u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}) & =f(x) \\\\ ({u}_{1}^{'}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{''}+{u}_{2}^{'}{y}_{2}^{'}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{''})+p(x)({u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{'})+q(x)({u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}) & =f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}({y}_{1}^{''}+p(x){y}_{1}^{'}+q(x){y}_{1})+{u}_{2}({y}_{2}^{''}+p(x){y}_{2}^{'}+q(x){y}_{2})+{u}_{1}^{'}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}^{'}{y}_{2}^{'} & =f(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $y_1'' + p(x) y_1' + q(x)y_1=0$ and $y_2'' + p(x) y_2' + q(x) y_2=0$ since $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the corresponding homogeneous ODE. Thus, we get\n\n$$\nu_1' y_1' + u_2' y_2' = f(x).\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{'}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{'}{y}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{1}^{'}{y}_{1}^{'}+{u}_{2}^{'}{y}_{2}^{'} &=f\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the solution of the system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{'} & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}0 & {y}_{2} \\\\ f & {y}_{2}^{'}\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{'} & {y}_{2}^{'}\\end{aligned}|}=-\\frac{{y}_{2}f}{{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}}=-\\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{'} & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & 0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{'} & f\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{'} & {y}_{2}^{'}\\end{aligned}|}=\\frac{{y}_{1}f}{{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{'}}=\\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $W(y_1,y_2)$ is the Wronskian.\n\nIntegrating, we reach the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186230244 #edb/ref 1065 4152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230244 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5ca38230-bd47-5c8e-a406-3348da7a971e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230244 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278629 #edb/ref 1080 "-20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278629 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278629 #edb/re