92186279516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281055 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "797a794d-3c44-5cfd-9959-01ae83cb5934" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281160 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186278797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281160 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281160 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281160 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281160 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281160 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281160 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281160 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186280179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281160 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b9a1c281-1dcb-592f-8bb1-786e3c4e7bdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281175 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186278629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281175 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186278665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281175 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186278735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281175 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281175 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281175 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281175 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281175 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186278665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281175 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c42f751a-ec9a-5f92-8bd2-846f2ed3dd93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281548 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281548 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162790" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281548 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281548 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=x-{y}^{2}$ given that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b349e078-f370-57a4-8287-923743beec39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281548 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y &=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda x &=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y(1+\\lambda ) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system gives \n\n$$\n-x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n \nThen, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 2,0),\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{17}{4}$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(2,0),$ and its maximum value is $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186576411] [17592186308984 #edb/ref 1080 "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{3x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308984 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308984 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1f063173-e6fe-5d2a-99b7-378065d2b1d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309242 #edb/ref 1080 "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{5}){e}^{-4x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309242 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309242 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40cef204-8013-5a17-b8d9-ae936e8062e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309538 #edb/ref 1080 "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309538 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309538 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6700055d-6c65-5ea7-bc2b-4856d3527804" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309542 #edb/ref 1080 "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{7}){e}^{-4x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309542 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309542 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "67a87526-29fc-5bf6-8e12-907f098b41ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309834 #edb/ref 1080 "-({x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{3}){e}^{-4x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309834 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309834 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8a51098b-f1b1-5736-8996-e4133727d23f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311319 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186308984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311319 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311319 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311319 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311319 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311319 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311319 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311319 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186309538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311319 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b5b23d5d-5883-57bd-8005-c893ed0c9ce4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311858 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311858 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311858 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-12y=14x{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{-7x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311858 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0163f286-fc30-51ed-907e-500011ffd762" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311858 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′} &=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=14x{e}^{-4x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-4x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x})^{′}-({e}^{-4x})^{′}({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-4x})(3{e}^{3x})-(-4{e}^{-4x})({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-x}+4{e}^{-x} \\\\  & =7{e}^{-x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions  ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent. \n\nLet's compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{3x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{14x{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{-4x}}{7{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int x{e}^{-7x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2})({e}^{-4x})+[-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-7x}]({e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{-4x}-\\frac{2}{49}(7x+1){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}+(-{x}^{2}-\\frac{2x}{7}-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =(A-\\frac{2}{49}){e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we redefine the arbitrary constant $A$ as\n\n$$\nA:=(A-\\frac{2}{49})\n$$\n\nour general solution becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x}+B{e}^{3x}\\underbrace{-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}}_{g(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=-({x}^{2}+\\frac{2x}{7}){e}^{-4x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186576411] [17592186360926 #edb/ref 1080 "54\\,\\text{in}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360926 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360926 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1609399a-f596-5865-ab9c-6aadc92d23db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360976 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360976 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360976 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "20c1fa27-09bb-576b-97dd-85dd401dc8a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361019 #edb/ref 1080 "54{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361019 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361019 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "294a6497-3763-569f-bdf9-42356cd00564" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361102 #edb/ref 1080 "42\\,\\text{in}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361102 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361102 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3a4ae6f5-4806-5c92-95fb-a3ec3dd390a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361202 #edb/ref 1080 "32{\\text{ cm}}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361202 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361202 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "52b8263c-0e8f-52be-ba17-ae87e84b31f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361204 #edb/ref 1080 "48{\\text{ cm